AUDIO: 'This strange-looking glass tube does something that almost no solar panel in the United States is currently capable of. Inside the vacuum tube is a strip of photovoltaic cells that generate electricity like a regular panel, while simultaneously heating'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water for the building using a completely different mechanism. But this ingenious design doesn\'t just overcome one of the biggest drawbacks of modern solar energy. It also allows the entire system to capture approximately four times'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more solar energy than a standard photovoltaic panel. This is Naked Energy Virtu, and it\'s just one of the PVT systems that are currently changing the solar energy landscape in Europe. While British and Dutch'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'companies are already installing it on libraries, hotels, and thousands of homes, the technology has barely made it to American rooftops. So how does it actually work? And will we see this in homes in the States anytime soon? Although solar'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'photovoltaics have excellent advantages, this technology also has significant disadvantages. A standard silicon solar panel converts 15 to 22% of the sunlight that hits it into electricity. And the rest, almost 80%, does'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'n\'t disappear anywhere. It is converted into heat, which is absorbed by the dark cells and the glass above them. And this is a huge problem. You see, silicon "doesn\'t like" heat. Every solar cell has what'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineers call a temperature coefficient. For crystalline panels on almost every roof, it is between minus 0.33 and minus 0.5% for every degree Celsius above 25°C. This is a'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'laboratory standard, but no working panel stays that cool. In the sun, the panel on the roof heats up 20–30 ° C more than the surrounding air,'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which on a clear summer day heats it up to 60 ° C and above. If you calculate, the panel can lose 10 to 15% of its rated power just due to heating. This creates an unusual'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'paradox. The brighter and hotter the day, the more electricity you expect, but the panel loses it precisely when the load on the network is greatest and the air conditioner turns on. The energy is right on your roof,'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but the panel simply can\'t use it. And what\'s worse, the heat is harmful to it. But there is a new technology that, instead of wasting this heat, uses it and can increase'

[02:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the efficiency of a traditional solar panel from 20% to over 70%. This technology is called photovoltaic thermal solar energy, and it is a logical evolution of traditional solar systems on our rooftops . You take solar'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cells that produce electricity and attach a circuit with a liquid to the back of them. The fluid performs two functions in one pass. It transfers the waste heat to where it is useful: to the hot water tank or'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heating circuit. And by removing this heat from the cells, it cools them. Colder cells are more efficient cells. So, the same action that collects heat also increases the output of electricity. The problem has always been'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'implementation. After all, a solar panel is a multilayer structure made of glass, silicon, and a plastic substrate. And each of these materials expands and contracts at different rates when heated and cooled. If you attach a hot liquid system to'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'such a panel, the mismatch could destroy it from the inside. The first hybrid panels from various startups did just that. They were peeling off. They were losing contact between the layers. And their polymer components were destroyed by'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ultraviolet light over the years. And because the certification field treated solar power and solar thermal as two completely different categories, these hybrids fell out of sight, with no standard to verify them. But now there'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are several European companies that are making real progress in this area. Each of them has its own unique systems that are revolutionizing the European solar industry. The first is the British company Naked Energy, founded'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'near Crawley, UK, in 2009 by Christoph Williams and Nicholas Simmons. They created it specifically to solve the heat problem that the rest of the solar world ignored. They'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'applied for patents in 2012, received them in 2018, and refined the design at pilot sites in Malta and across Europe before launching it on the market. Their product, Virtue PV, is a series of horizontal glass tubes, each'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of which is a sealed cylinder of borosilicate glass about 7 inches in diameter . The air inside is pumped out, replaced by a vacuum. The same trick used in a thermos to keep coffee'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hot for hours. Inside this vacuum is a strip of highly efficient monocrystalline cells—the same 22 percent cells you\'d find on a good roof panel— attached to an aluminum plate . Copper'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'channels pass through the plate, through which a mixture of water and glycol flows . Removing air reduces heat loss to the environment to almost zero. Therefore, the tube continues to produce useful heat even on a gloomy winter'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'day, when a regular uninsulated panel would simply release its heat into the sky. Cells generate electricity from light. The liquid removes waste heat from their back side, and almost nothing is lost. Naked Energy'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'estimates an overall efficiency of 80%: about 20% as electricity and 60% as heat, compared to the 20% you get from a standard panel that only produces electricity. Because the tubes'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are placed low and flat on the roof with integrated reflectors between them —without gaps or slanted frames that catch the wind— they cover over 85% of the roof area, while conventional systems'

[05:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only cover half. In October 2024, the British Library in London launched the largest solar thermal installation in the UK. 950 Virtu PVT collectors on over 700 square meters of the roof of a building'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that has the status of a first-degree architectural monument. It provides the library with hot water and heating, which was previously done by burning natural gas. This is expected'

[05:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to reduce the building\'s carbon emissions by 55 tons each year. In a rare application of solar energy, the same system helps maintain precise temperature and humidity levels that protect the national collection of more than 170 million'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exhibits. Currently, Naked Energy is developing its tubes primarily as a solution for large buildings with significant hot water costs. But a completely different company took the same idea and aimed it directly at regular homes. This'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'company is Triple Solar, founded in Amsterdam in 2009 by Kees van Mager and Terrence van Buuren. While Naked Energy focused on hotels and hospitals, Triple Solar focused on residential buildings in the'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Netherlands. Its design looks much closer to the panels we already use. From the front, it looks like a regular photovoltaic panel with monocrystalline cells. On the reverse side, it is an'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open aluminum heat exchanger, intentionally uninsulated and left open to the weather. The reason for this is that the Triple Solar panel tries to capture heat from more than just the sun. It receives'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'heat from three sources simultaneously: direct sunlight, diffused daylight on a cloudy day, and the surrounding air. Because it collects heat from the air by convection, similar to the outdoor unit of a'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conventional heat pump, the panel continues to operate 24/7—through clouds, darkness, and down to air temperatures of around minus 10°C. The refrigerant circuit passes through a heat exchanger, picks up'

[07:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'low-potential heat from both the air and the back of the cells, and transfers it indoors to a compact heat pump that converts it into heating and hot water. Removing heat from the cells increases their annual'

[07:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electricity generation by 5–10% compared to an uncooled panel. When calculated across the entire system, Triple Solar achieves a total solar energy utilization of over 70%. Paired with a properly'

[07:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'selected heat pump, it achieves a seasonal coefficient of performance of up to 5.6 for heating. In simple terms, this means that for every unit of electricity consumed, the system produces more than five units of heat. There is no'

[07:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fan bolted to the outside wall and no compressor operating in the open air, which is a huge advantage. In the densely built-up areas of the Netherlands, an external heat pump means noise. And noise means a quarrel with neighbors. So a'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'silent rooftop system, which allows you to do without an outdoor unit altogether, became an obvious solution. Triple Solar teamed up with German firm Consolar in 2016 to develop the current generation of panels, and they are now'

[08:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'installed in over 6,000 apartments and buildings across the Netherlands. In January 2023, Dutch pension giant ABP invested €10 million in the company for further scaling. So,'

[08:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Europe clearly supports the PVT energy movement. However, in the US, it is almost impossible to find such a thing in homes . So why has n\'t it caught on in America yet? The answer lies in a'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number of reasons. The first of these is that this technology is difficult to produce and is currently expensive. Ensuring a long-lasting vacuum seal between the glass and metal, precisely connecting the cells, laying thin copper channels—all of'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this requires slow, careful, and clean assembly that is not yet comparable in cost to a conventional panel coming off an automated gigawatt line. Then—cold. Such non-glass panels can become'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'frosted at temperatures below 4° C, and the ice blocks light and reduces efficiency until smart defrost cycles and special coatings clear them. In addition, a PVT panel is simply heavier than a regular one because it contains an absorbent'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'plate and liquid, which on an old roof under winter snow may mean the need for structural reinforcement even before the first panel is installed . Installation also requires a contractor who is well -versed in both high-voltage'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'electrical work and pressurized plumbing work . And because construction specialties are so diverse, homeowners often have to hire two separate companies for the same job, which inflates the cost before anyone even gets on the ladder. But'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the deepest reason why PVT hasn\'t made it into American homes has nothing to do with the hardware. The fact is that the pressure that stimulates the development of technology in Europe is almost absent here. Across the continent, regulations have gradually changed against'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noisy outdoor fan units . In the Netherlands, the noise level of new heat pumps at night on the border with a neighbor\'s property was limited to 40 decibels. In Germany, the limit for residential areas is 35. At the same time, German legislation'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requires that every new heating system receives at least 65% of its energy from renewable sources . Caught between noise restrictions and renewable energy policies, a silent'

[10:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'rooftop energy system has ceased to be a luxury and has become one of the few options that meet regulations. America did not have similar pressure. Cheaper energy, a more relaxed gas policy, and significantly'

[10:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more space to place the fan where the neighbors won\'t hear it. However, PVT is already reaching America, just not in the way people expect. Naked Energy launched its first project in the'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'United States in late 2023. 240 of its collectors are installed in a Creighton University dormitory in Omas, Nebraska; they heat water for 400 students and reduce the building\'s carbon emissions by 40'

[10:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tons per year. Its distribution partner, ELM Solar, invested $3 million in a factory in Dallas, Texas, to manufacture these tubes on American soil, with the goal of capturing the entire North American'

[10:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'market and avoiding import duties. And this path, where commercial buildings go first, is not a delay. This is the plan. Costs are reduced by scaling up to hotels, universities, and'

[11:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'apartment buildings—structures with sufficient heat demand to justify the price—and only then does the technology become affordable for the average home. This is exactly the same path that conventional solar energy took to become'

[11:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mainstream. However, there are still obstacles to the widespread adoption of PVT systems in America. The main one is that in July 2025, a new federal law eliminated the 30% tax credit for'

[11:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solar panel owners at the end of the year and put the commercial credit on its own countdown tied to construction timelines and new restrictions on foreign components. This makes the timeline tighter, but at the same time highlights'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'why the “ commerce first, made in America” approach is important. A tube made in Texas for a hotel has a much better chance of withstanding this pressure than an imported panel for a private home. So the PVT technology that may eventually make'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'its way to your roof will almost certainly end up there after it is widely deployed and used in commercial buildings. Just look at the roofs of commercial buildings , and when you see strange tubes on an'

[12:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'increasing number of such structures, know that this technology will soon be available at an affordable price for your home. Here at Wildline, we are drawn to solutions that the mainstream has missed, and PVT is a prime example. A'

[12:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'solution to losses so commonplace that almost no one stops to question it. A panel that loses 80% of its solar energy has never been a perfect product. It was simply the version that became cheap first,'

[12:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Europe spent 15 years quietly developing a better alternative. If it\'s important to you to understand where home energy is really headed, subscribing and sharing is the easiest way to ensure that such knowledge continues to find'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
